-- | Client access passes: the on-ledger whitelist of Exodus.
--
-- Only approved clients may use Exodus (subscribe to the fund, send and
-- receive tokens through our transfer factories, and later split and trade).
-- When the platform admin approves Alice, the Operator creates ONE
-- 'ClientAccess' contract for her. Client actions take passes and check them:
--
--   Alice subscribes 500 USDC
--     -> Subscribe gets Alice's pass
--     -> checkAccess: "is this a pass from OUR operator, for Alice?"  yes
--     -> the subscription goes ahead
--
--   Alice sends 50 USYC to Bob
--     -> the transfer factory gets Alice's pass AND Bob's pass
--     -> both must be valid, so tokens only move between approved clients
--        (like the real, permissioned USYC; spec gap 11)
--
-- Why one pass per client instead of a 'users : [Party]' list on each shared
-- contract (the fund, the factories, the price feed)? See docs/client-app.md:
--   - privacy: with a list, every client sees every other client's name.
--     With passes, Alice sees only her own pass.
--   - no contention: approving Carol does not archive and recreate the fund,
--     so Bob's subscribe at the same moment does not fail.
--   - revoke: archiving one pass removes all of Carol's access at once.
--
-- Clients no longer see the shared contracts at all. The app hands them to
-- the client's command through "explicit disclosure" (see spec section 8.A).
module Exodus.Access where


-- | One approved client.
-- Example: ClientAccess(operator = Operator, client = Alice, issuers = [UsycIssuer, UsdcIssuer]).
template ClientAccess
  with
    operator : Party    -- the platform that approved the client (the Operator demo party)
    client   : Party    -- the approved client, for example Alice or "alice-7f3a::1220..."
    issuers  : [Party]  -- token issuers that check this pass on transfers, for example [UsycIssuer, UsdcIssuer]
  where
    -- The operator vouches for the client. The client cannot make its own pass.
    signatory operator

    -- WHO SEES THE PASS, and why:
    --   client:  it has to pass its own pass id into its commands.
    --   issuers: when Alice sends USYC to Bob, the UsycIssuer's transfer
    --            factory must read BOB's pass. In Daml a choice may only
    --            fetch a contract if one of the parties authorizing it can see
    --            that contract. Alice cannot see Bob's pass, but the issuer
    --            (who signs the factory) can. Real issuers do KYC, so knowing
    --            their client list is normal.
    -- Other clients do NOT see it, so no client can list who else is a client.
    observer client, issuers

    -- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- Revoke: the operator removes the client's access. From then on every
    -- action with this pass fails, because the pass no longer exists. The
    -- client's tokens stay theirs; they just cannot use Exodus, and nobody
    -- can send them tokens through our factories.
    -- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    choice Revoke : ()
      controller operator
      do
        pure ()


-- | Fail unless 'accessCid' is a pass from 'operator' for 'client'.
--
-- Called inside client choices, for example 'Subscribe'. Examples that fail:
--   - Bob passes Alice's pass                   -> "access pass belongs to another client"
--   - Alice makes her own pass (operator = Alice) -> "access pass is not from this operator"
--   - Alice's pass was revoked                   -> the fetch fails (the contract is gone)
checkAccess : Party -> Party -> ContractId ClientAccess -> Update ()
checkAccess operator client accessCid = do
  access <- fetch accessCid
  assertMsg "access pass is not from this operator" (access.operator == operator)
  assertMsg "access pass belongs to another client" (access.client == client)
